"""Narration for the video on C. S. Wu's cobalt-60 experiment (parity violation in beta decay).
All drawings are made in chalk; no paper figures are used.

  python prep.py tts      -> audio/sNN.wav (sends the narration text to Microsoft's online TTS)
"""
import asyncio, os, subprocess, sys, wave

HERE = os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__))
AUD = os.path.join(HERE, 'audio')

NARR = [
"Look into a mirror. The world you see there looks strange, but possible. Left and right are swapped, yet nothing in it seems to break the laws of physics. For a long time, physicists were sure of this. They called it the conservation of parity. Then, in the winter of 1956, an experiment with radioactive cobalt, cooled almost to absolute zero, showed that nature can tell left from right. This is the story of that experiment, led by Chien-Shiung Wu.",
"What does parity conservation mean? Take any experiment, and build its mirror image. If parity is conserved, the mirror experiment gives the mirror result. Then no experiment can tell you whether you are watching the real world, or its reflection. Gravity passes this test. Electromagnetism passes it. So does the strong force, which holds nuclei together. So everyone assumed that the fourth force, the weak force, which causes beta decay, would pass it too. It seemed too natural to question. But nobody had actually checked.",
"The trouble started with two particles, called theta and tau. Theta decayed into two pions. Tau decayed into three pions. A pion has negative parity. So two pions have positive parity, and three pions have negative parity. If parity is conserved, theta and tau must be two different particles. But they had exactly the same mass, and the same lifetime. This was the tau theta puzzle. In 1956, Tsung-Dao Lee and Chen-Ning Yang asked a bold question. What if it is one particle, and the weak force simply does not conserve parity? They searched the literature, and found that no experiment had ever tested this. So they proposed some. Today we know they were right. Theta and tau are the same particle: the kaon.",
"How do you test mirror symmetry? You need something that looks different in a mirror. Take a spinning nucleus. Its spin points along its rotation axis, say upward. Now suppose that, when it decays, it sends its electron mostly downward, opposite to the spin. Look at this in a mirror standing beside it. In the mirror, the nucleus rotates the other way, so its spin points down. But the electron still goes down. In the real world, electrons leave opposite to the spin. In the mirror world, they leave along the spin. The two worlds are different. So the test is simple to state. Line up the spins of many nuclei, and count the electrons going up, and going down. If parity is conserved, the two numbers must be equal.",
"Lee and Yang suggested cobalt 60. It decays into nickel 60, by emitting an electron and an antineutrino. The nickel then gives off two gamma rays. Cobalt 60 has a large spin, 5 units, so it behaves like a tiny magnet that can be lined up. But here is the hard part. At room temperature, heat shakes the nuclei, and their spins point in random directions. And a nuclear magnet is very weak. To line the spins up, you need a strong magnetic field, and a temperature of about one hundredth of a degree above absolute zero. Wu was an expert on beta decay, at Columbia University. For the cold, she teamed up with a low temperature group at the National Bureau of Standards in Washington: Ernest Ambler, Raymond Hayward, Dale Hoppes, and Ralph Hudson.",
"Here is the setup. A thin layer of cobalt 60 was grown on the surface of a crystal of a special salt, cerium magnesium nitrate. The crystal sat inside a cryostat, cooled by liquid helium to about one degree above absolute zero. To go lower, they used a trick called adiabatic demagnetization. First, a strong magnet lines up the electron magnets in the salt, and the helium carries away the heat that this releases. Then the crystal is isolated, and the magnet is switched off. The salt cools itself, down to about 0.003 kelvin. Now a coil applies a small vertical field. The electrons of each cobalt atom turn it into a huge field at the nucleus, and the cobalt spins line up. Just above the cobalt sits a small scintillator, which flashes each time an electron hits it. A long plastic rod carries the light out to a detector. Two more counters outside watch the gamma rays. They show how well the nuclei are lined up.",
"Then they counted. The field can point up, or down, so the cobalt spins can point toward the electron counter, or away from it. If parity is conserved, the counter should not care. But it did. With the spins pointing away from the counter, the count rate was about twenty percent higher than normal. With the spins pointing toward the counter, it was about twenty percent lower. Electrons prefer to leave opposite to the nuclear spin. And there was a beautiful check. The crystal slowly warms up. Over several minutes, the spins become random again, and the two count rates drift back together, just as the gamma ray signal fades. The effect follows the polarization. Parity is not conserved in beta decay.",
"Why does nature do this? The answer lies in handedness. Think of a particle as a spinning bullet. If it spins like a right handed screw moving forward, we call it right handed. If it spins the other way, left handed. The weak force acts on left handed particles, and on right handed antiparticles. In cobalt decay, the nuclear spin drops from 5 to 4. The electron and the antineutrino carry away the missing unit, so both of their spins point along the cobalt spin. The antineutrino is right handed, so it flies off along the spin. The electron is left handed, so it flies the opposite way. That is exactly what Wu saw. And the violation is not small. It is as large as it can possibly be.",
"The news spread fast. Within days, Richard Garwin, Leon Lederman, and Marcel Weinrich confirmed it at Columbia's cyclotron, using the decays of pions and muons. Both papers appeared together, in February 1957. Many physicists were shocked. Wolfgang Pauli had been ready to bet a large sum that the experiment would find nothing. That same year, Lee and Yang received the Nobel Prize. Wu did not share it, which many physicists have regretted ever since. She later received the first Wolf Prize in physics. The experiment also showed that the symmetry between matter and antimatter, called C, is broken. For a while, the combination, C P, seemed safe. But in 1964, that was found to be broken too, by a small amount.",
"Let us sum up. Parity conservation says that the mirror image of any experiment is also a possible experiment. A puzzle with two particles led Lee and Yang to doubt this for the weak force. Wu and her colleagues lined up cobalt 60 nuclei, at a few thousandths of a degree above absolute zero, and counted the electrons. More electrons came out opposite to the nuclear spin. So the mirror world is different from ours. The weak force knows left from right. And it took one careful, very cold experiment to find out. Thanks for watching.",
]


async def tts(voice='en-US-AvaNeural', rate='-6%'):
    import edge_tts
    os.makedirs(AUD, exist_ok=True)
    for i, t in enumerate(NARR, 1):
        mp3 = os.path.join(AUD, 's%02d.mp3' % i); wav = os.path.join(AUD, 's%02d.wav' % i)
        await edge_tts.Communicate(t, voice, rate=rate).save(mp3)
        subprocess.run(['ffmpeg', '-y', '-loglevel', 'error', '-i', mp3, '-ar', '22050', '-ac', '1', '-c:a', 'pcm_s16le', wav], check=True)
        with wave.open(wav) as w: print('s%02d: %.1f s' % (i, w.getnframes() / w.getframerate()), flush=True)


if __name__ == '__main__':
    if len(sys.argv) > 1 and sys.argv[1] == 'tts': asyncio.run(tts())
    else: print(__doc__)
